// A STATIC extension method reached through an INSTANCE binds its labels the
// same way it does through the type.
//
// `Bag.solo(index: 3)` works. `b.solo(index: 4)` reports ``solo` has no
// parameter named `index`` — with ONE method declared, so no overload set and
// no tie-break is involved. The instance path strips a `self` slot the callee
// does not have, so every label lines up against the wrong parameter and the
// last one falls off the end; where the labels happen to bind, codegen then
// passes the receiver as argument 0 and fails the verifier
// (`Type of #1 arg mismatch: i64 != %"Bag"`).
//
// Found while fixing DF-217e, whose pin originally called its statics this way.
// The two are independent: DF-217e was the DECLARATION-site identity test
// (`_overload_cand_offset` on both sides now), and this is the CALL-site
// parameter offset, `expressions.py`'s `param_offset = 1 if not
// method_info.is_init else 0`, which owes the same `is_static` term. Fixing it
// means deciding whether an instance may name a static at all — the alternative
// reading is a clean refusal pointing at `Bag.solo(...)` — so it wants a ruling
// rather than a patch.
// XFAIL: DF-217q a static extension method called through an instance mis-binds its labels, because the call-site parameter offset skips a `self` slot the callee does not have
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// type 3
// instance 4

struct Bag { n: Int }

extension Bag {
    func solo(index: Int) -> Int { index }
}

func main() {
    print("type {Bag.solo(index: 3)}")
    let b = Bag(n: 0)
    print("instance {b.solo(index: 4)}")
}
